means to our offering,

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:08.840
and why it is such an
important piece of what

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.930
we do in Google Cloud.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:17.440
So you are very familiar
with containers.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.930
You know that we've
been working on--

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.020
and the industry
in open source has

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.970
been working on
containerized applications,

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.105
and that allows us to
extract the infrastructure

00:01:31.105 --> 00:01:33.130
of resources.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.180
And Kubernetes really has
become in that journey

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:43.160
the de facto orchestrator for
the containerized applications.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.850
So it has many advantages.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.590
It allows to modernize
applications.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:53.620
It provides the portability
so that you can actually

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:54.950
develop your applications.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.110
And then you can port and deploy
very fundamental on how we

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.260
as Google see the public cloud.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:08.860
And our view that it should be a
multi-cloud, it should be open,

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.330
it should be portable.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:16.940
So why Kubernetes has
been so successful?

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.530
So it really provides
the capabilities

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.160
of not only orchestrating your
applications and your workload

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.260
spot, it provides
certain properties

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.710
that make it very appealing.

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:34.340
So you have a stable
IP for your services.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.940
You're able to deploy your
applications within the cluster

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.910
and then provide a stable
IP and stable communication

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:41.870
to the external world.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.070
But internally, the
pod IPs and the pods

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.125
that are used for serving that
application are ephemeral.

00:02:50.125 --> 00:02:51.500
So that gives you
the flexibility

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.410
while serving the applications
to the external world

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.720
it is stable.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.000
Now it provides you as
well with the flexibility

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.640
of delivering Layer 4 services,
delivering increased services

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.510
through our Layer
7 capabilities.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:15.480
We as the implementation
of Kubernetes

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:21.680
have been delivering pod
IPs as first class citizens

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.790
so that they are
routable to the pods.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.690
Now, as part of your
deployments as well,

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.260
you have the capability
of having policies.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:38.570
And then you're able to declare
that certain applications are

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.100
able to communicate with others.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:46.190
And those are network policies
as well as standardized APIs

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:50.570
that you can use for
declaring your intentions

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.250
of communicating
your applications.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.890
And then you can do so as
well within the cluster.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.690
Sometimes, certain
applications are

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:04.310
exposed to the external world,
some others that are only

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.945
made available internally,
and only through the pods

00:04:08.945 --> 00:04:11.300
from the applications
that are meaningful

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.020
to that communication.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:22.110
So now we as Google Cloud
have been delivering

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.480
solutions for Kubernetes.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.540
Kubernetes has been a game
changer for developers.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.920
It really provides
the de facto way

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.490
of modernizing applications.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.730
Now obviously, it's been very
successful for developers

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.980
because all these
flexibility and portability

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.460
that it provides.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:49.110
Now the issue is when you are
delivering these applications

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:53.460
and you are doing it for
production environments,

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:57.780
you still have the Day-2
operations to care about.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.600
So you need to care about
managing the components.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.610
You need to care about upgrading
the nodes in the cluster.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.640
You need to care
about how to scale

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.850
in, providing high
availability, auto repairs.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:18.060
And therefore, we feel that
part of the problem statement

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.610
has not really been
solved with Kubernetes.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:26.870
So now what we have
been working really

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:31.520
hard in the last few
years is delivering

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.310
the solution for the
second part of the problem.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.310
Now we want to deliver
a solution that

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:45.590
is providing the manageability
of the cluster with upgrades,

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:49.910
with auto repairs,
with auto scaling.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:53.450
And that is really
the beauty of GKE.

00:05:53.450 --> 00:06:00.520
It is really the solution
not only for your developers,

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.380
but also for the system
platform operators.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.760
It really takes the
journey of Kubernetes

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:14.730
is from developer tool to
an enterprise-ready solution

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:19.470
to be deployed for your
production environments.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:27.100
Now, as you can see, we've
been delivering solutions

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.410
for high availability
so then you

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:34.660
can have SLAs as part
of your deployments.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.910
For example, we've been
providing high availability

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:39.970
not only for the cluster,
but also for the master--

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.830
with regional master so that you
can have availability in data

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.410
plane, in control plane.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.240
We provide auto patching.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:56.620
For example, taking care of
security vulnerability issues.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:00.400
We provide auto repair.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.380
We provide auto scaling.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:04.030
We provide, for
example, solutions

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.410
for managed TLS certificates.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:13.510
So you can think of it,
a complete end-to-end,

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.530
enterprise-ready solution.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:24.030
And we really see this as a
fundamental platform for us

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.670
to deliver the capabilities
of our cloud to our customer.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:32.710
So the growth of GKE in Google
Cloud has been enormous.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:36.060
And we'll just see more and
more enterprises really taking

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:39.240
advantage of the
agility of containers

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.400
combined with the manageability
of the platform itself,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.410
and the security
capabilities, the auto

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.880
manage capabilities that
the platform provides.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.860
But the journey
does not end there.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.620
We've been very busy
adding more value.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.524
What is that value?

00:08:04.524 --> 00:08:11.240
As we are delivering GKE as the
game changer for the platform

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:18.650
operator, we are doing so
not only on our platform,

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:22.390
but also for your
on-prem environment.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:29.620
It's been really something
that many enterprises have

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.520
been asking us to do.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.409
They want to see the
capabilities of GKE--

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:42.390
the manageability, the
readiness, the security--

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.460
not only for the
cloud environment,

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.980
but also for the
on-prem environments.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:50.280
We're going to have
Scott and Paul talking

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.870
about why that is important.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:55.890
And there are many reasons
why enterprises might not

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:59.530
be able to deploy certain
applications in the cloud.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:06.460
But what we are doing is
providing the same principles

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:10.370
that we have in our
cloud in on-prem.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:11.980
So what does that mean?

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.430
We mean that we are
able to provide clusters

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:21.820
that are able to be grouped and
able to be control and managed.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.040
There is an
administration cluster.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:27.280
There are control
plane that rounds

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.930
on-prem so that you actually
have a standalone operating

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:36.670
cluster on-prem whether there
is connectivity or not to cloud.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:43.420
But it gets
automatically started up.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:49.030
It has connectivity with the
registry that runs as well

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:51.270
locally so that it starts up.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:56.050
And then it gets reused
there in the cloud

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.740
so that we have
the credentials, we

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:05.020
have the security on a single
pane of glass for visibility.

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:06.970
It shows in our cloud console.

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:09.490
And then you're able
to really manage

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:11.680
the functions of
your cluster as you

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.510
will do if it is in GKE in GCP.

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:18.610
Now it is important,
again, to note

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:22.630
that we understand that
the deployment needs

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:24.900
to be operating in on-prem.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.550
So we've been very
careful of offering

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:32.380
the capabilities of running
autonomously in on-prem,

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:36.940
even though it has a
register to the cloud

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:41.590
for manageability reasons so
that we can do Up 2 upgrades.

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:46.900
We can actually
manage the cluster.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.840
And the boring part is over.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:54.120
So more interesting, I want
to welcome on stage Paul.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.750
Paul Ravello is in HSBC.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:59.710
He's been a very trusted
partner in all the journey.

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.720
And he is going
to talk more about

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:05.035
why this is important for
their deployments in HSBC.

00:11:08.385 --> 00:11:09.260
PAUL REVELLO: Thanks.

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:10.343
INES ENVID: Welcome, Paul.

00:11:10.343 --> 00:11:14.162
PAUL REVELLO: Thank you, Ines.

00:11:14.162 --> 00:11:15.596
So hey, guys.

00:11:15.596 --> 00:11:17.825
My name is Paul
[AUDIO OUT] HSBC.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:21.330
On now?

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:22.380
Yeah.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:23.380
Hey, guys.

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:24.390
My name is Paul.

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:28.740
I work at HSBC, an enterprise
cloud architect there.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:32.580
team is responsible for
helping to set the cloud

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.590
strategy at the bank.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:36.720
Typically, that means
public cloud for us.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.440
But we are now building out
internal cloud capabilities

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.680
as well, and we've
settled on Kubernetes

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.840
to help us achieve that.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.860
A little bit about HSBC.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.420
We're a very large organization.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:53.720
We're spread out globally.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:56.810
Large number of
IT professionals,

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:58.865
and we have all
of the challenges

00:11:58.865 --> 00:12:02.510
that that typically brings
in large enterprises

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:05.360
when it comes to
infrastructure, provisioning,

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:09.140
application development,
application deployment, patch

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:09.890
management.

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:14.870
So when we started
looking at this stuff,

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:21.260
we really had a list of items
that kind of have a long lead

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:25.353
time typically at the bank, and
we really wanted to fix them.

00:12:25.353 --> 00:12:27.020
So if you look at a
standard application

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:31.460
deployment in our
enterprise, it's VM-based.

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:34.650
VM-based applications
require VMs to be deployed.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:37.130
We have an IS
capability on-premise,

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:41.150
but it's not necessarily
a rapid provisioning.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:44.300
It still has a lead
time associated with it.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.470
It's a very static
type of provisioning.

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.650
You ask for something,
you get that something.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:49.940
There's no real leeway there.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:51.440
If you need something
else, you have

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:55.010
to ask for something else
later on down the road.

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:57.920
Moving to Kubernetes
and GKE On-Prem,

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:01.460
we can have pre-built
infrastructure that supports

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:02.940
the Kubernetes environment.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.630
And then we can give
people the ability

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:09.410
to create images that they
can deploy there and not have

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:12.320
to wait for infrastructure
to be provisioned to do that.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:14.900
That's a very big win for us.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:18.260
It gives us the ability
to really rapidly change

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:20.300
the posture there.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:23.600
IP allocation is a
problem that we normally

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:27.170
have on-premise when it comes
to infrastructure provisioning.

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.210
The reason that's an
issue for us is it's

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:30.950
a little bit of
a manual process,

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:32.820
but it's also a static thing.

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:37.120
IPs are tied to many things
in a typical VM application

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:37.620
deployment.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.600
They're tied to DNS names.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:43.100
They're tied to maybe
licensing, whatever it might be.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:47.360
There's a one-to-one mapping
there of something usually.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:50.420
When we go to Kubernetes and
we're running in island mode

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.640
with GKE On-Premise
specifically,

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:55.400
it gives us the ability
to have a wild card DNS

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.190
entry and a single
VIP on a load balancer

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:01.220
that all of our applications can
target that are running inside

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:02.460
of that infrastructure.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.840
So you provision it once,
and it's good to go.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:08.120
DNS, again, wildcard
to the cluster.

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:09.950
Same thing like
I just mentioned.

00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:13.290
Rather than a manual
provisioning process for DNS,

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:14.360
there's a wildcard entry.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:17.870
Makes things much easier to deal
with, much more faster, much

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:20.630
easier to deploy applications.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:22.790
Configuring service--
again, typically

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:25.490
if you think of the lifecycle
of a VM-based application

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:27.650
deployment, you request the VM.

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:30.320
You get some information about
that VM at some point in time--

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:33.290
an IP address, DNS name.

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:35.750
You then need to go
back to in second team

00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:38.660
and have them configure
a load balancer for you.

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:40.460
That's typically not
under your control

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:43.170
as an application developer
in a large enterprise.

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:45.350
That's something that
a separate team handles

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:47.150
on the infrastructure side.

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:51.558
This eliminates that because
you're now just using

00:14:51.558 --> 00:14:53.850
the ingress controller that's
built into the Kubernetes

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:54.775
environment.

00:14:54.775 --> 00:14:57.150
It's all handled as part of
your application deployment--

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.800
very simple, very
straightforward.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:02.450
Having a high availability
control plane.

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:05.290
This is an interesting
one for us.

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:06.530
We're in financial services.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.590
HA is very important to us.

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:11.080
Getting HA for every piece
of your infrastructure

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:14.540
that supports your application
deployments is a challenge.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:15.850
It's expensive.

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:18.320
Moving to Kubernetes kind
of solves that a little bit.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.560
And with GKE On-Premise
specifically, we

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.800
have the ability to have
highly available masters that

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:27.095
are separate from the actual
user clusters themselves.

00:15:27.095 --> 00:15:27.970
This is great for us.

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.478
It gives us the ability to scale
that independently, provide

00:15:30.478 --> 00:15:32.020
high availability
there independently

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:33.898
from the applications
that are running it.

00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:40.550
Day-2 operations-- Ines,
touched on this a little bit.

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:42.230
But this is a little
bit more specific

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:44.840
to the actual
application deployments.

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.880
So patches and
upgrades-- if you think

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:50.660
about VMware-based deployments,
how do you patch a VM?

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:52.190
You have to patch a VM, right?

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:57.130
Well, now you have 100,000 VMs,
you have to patch 100,000 VMs.

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.340
Upgrades-- same thing.

00:15:58.340 --> 00:15:59.780
You want to do an OS upgrade?

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:02.970
That's a major, major challenge
in a large enterprise.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:06.410
With GKE On-Premise--
Kubernetes, in general--

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:09.350
you can do customer-initiated
auto upgrades of the Kubernetes

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:11.600
environment that have already
been tested and approved

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:13.290
by Google.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:16.370
It's not something that
you need to necessarily do

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:17.307
automatically.

00:16:17.307 --> 00:16:18.140
We're an enterprise.

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:20.570
We wouldn't necessarily
want something

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.885
to upgrade automatically
behind the scenes for us,

00:16:22.885 --> 00:16:24.260
but it can be
customer-initiated.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:25.700
There's a button that
you can click to say

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:26.867
I want to upgrade right now.

00:16:26.867 --> 00:16:28.520
I want to upgrade at this time.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.320
Works very well for us.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.240
Reshaping infrastructure--
I touched on this before.

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:37.100
If you request a VM in a
typical enterprise, it's a VM.

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:38.510
If you need that changed--

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:40.220
if you need more
memory, more CPUs

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:42.660
allocated, more disk
space-- what do you do?

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:44.620
You have to submit
another request.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:46.460
It might be self-service,
but there's still

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:48.080
probably an approval
process there.

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:49.670
And there's a timing thing.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:52.130
Well, with
declarative intent, we

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.290
can put people in
a namespace that

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:56.168
has certain quotas
associated with it.

00:16:56.168 --> 00:16:58.460
They can deploy what they
want to their heart's content

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:01.100
in that namespace,
use their resources,

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:03.080
and reshape their
infrastructure that they're

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.839
running their application
in up to their quota.

00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:08.930
And the quota itself can
be a little bit easier

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:11.119
to enable self-service for.

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:12.829
Works very well, again.

00:17:12.829 --> 00:17:16.190
Resizing services-- in
a typical deployment,

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:18.950
with VMs, if you add
another VM to the service,

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:21.410
you have to contact that
load balancer team again,

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:23.359
have them add the IP
of the VM to the load

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:25.410
balancer for that application.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:26.599
It's not a seamless thing.

00:17:26.599 --> 00:17:28.310
It's not quick to do.

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:31.100
With auto scaling, that
happens naturally-- built

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:34.700
into Kubernetes.

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:37.700
control plane visibility-- this
is another really interesting

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:40.328
one for us.

00:17:40.328 --> 00:17:42.120
If you think about a
typical infrastructure

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.790
in an enterprise, many
different components,

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:48.090
many different vendors.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.040
Getting all of that
information in one place

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.280
typically relies
on another tool.

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:55.830
You either need some kind
of a sim or a log aggregator

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:59.400
to get all of that information
in one place to be able to use.

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:02.412
And even then, it's really
never perfect right.

00:18:02.412 --> 00:18:05.873
With GKE On-Prem, all of the
logs for the infrastructure

00:18:05.873 --> 00:18:07.790
that's supporting all
of the applications that

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:12.070
are running there
are sent very nicely

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.500
into Snapdragon over a tether.

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:16.350
Works very well.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:19.610
Troubleshooting and
logging-- a little bit more

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.950
like control plane
visibility, but this is

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:24.200
more on the application side.

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:25.850
In a typical
enterprise, it can be

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:30.230
very difficult to give
individual application

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:35.210
owners access to metrics related
directly to their applications.

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:37.670
In Kubernetes, in
GKE On-Prem, we

00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:41.390
have really advanced tooling
with Prometheus and Grafana.

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:43.310
It's very simple to
create custom dashboards

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:45.890
per application with
RBAC wrapped around them.

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:47.750
Individual application
teams can see

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:49.790
what they need to
see to understand

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:52.370
how their applications are
behaving from a CPU and memory

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:54.870
perspective, a
network perspective.

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:56.810
And we have Stackdriver
for actual application

00:18:56.810 --> 00:19:01.230
logs if that is what's
chosen to be utilized.

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:03.230
And as mentioned that
we've been working closely

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:05.940
on a lot of this
stuff, and we have.

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:08.523
And I think that, at
the end of the day,

00:19:08.523 --> 00:19:10.190
all of the work that
we've done together

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:12.230
has created a really
compelling package

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:15.530
to have an enterprise
deployment of Kubernetes that's

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:17.450
really scalable.

00:19:17.450 --> 00:19:19.700
It works really
well, and we're very

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:22.640
happy with all the integrations
that have been done so far.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:24.140
With that, I'm going
to hand it over

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:25.880
to Satya, who's
one of the people

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.010
that we've been working
very closely with.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:30.650
And he's been great
with us, working

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:33.000
on all of the networking
components inside the product.

00:19:38.458 --> 00:19:40.000
SATYADEEP MUSUVATHY:
Thank you, Paul.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:44.103
And I have Scott
joining me on stage too.

00:19:44.103 --> 00:19:46.270
So I'm going to start out
by thanking Paul and Scott

00:19:46.270 --> 00:19:50.470
because they've been
working with us from get-go,

00:19:50.470 --> 00:19:51.970
quantifying a lot
of these problems

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:54.760
and actually educating us a
lot on what actually works.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:56.410
Because when we
started GKE On-Prem,

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:59.080
we kind of recognized
that you understand

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:01.840
your network the best.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:03.520
It's not the cloud.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:06.160
You have your own constraints
on how you build the networks.

00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:07.993
You have your own
constraints on what works,

00:20:07.993 --> 00:20:10.040
and why it is the
way it is today.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.660
And our goal here was
to go in and actually

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:18.580
adapt GKE and Kubernetes to
work best in your environment

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:21.670
with minimal overhead in
terms of networking cost.

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.330
That was the way we
thought about the problem.

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:29.950
And HSBC has helped us a lot
in actually making that work

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:35.060
and making that actually
doable in some sense.

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:38.830
And so when we started
out, what we did was

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:40.750
we had to start with
some principles.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:43.010
How do we think
about this problem?

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:47.900
And when we did it in terms of
these five or six principles.

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:49.540
So the first thing
we wanted to do

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:52.240
was we wanted to support
native Kubernetes

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:54.140
constructs as much as possible.

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:56.800
So we didn't want to
go in and say, oh,

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.800
because you want to expose
something as a web service,

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.860
go do this other thing after
you deploy Kubernetes service.

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:06.310
We wanted you to be able
to use Kubernetes Ingress.

00:21:06.310 --> 00:21:09.670
We didn't want to make anything
that was Google-specific

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:11.500
or GKE On-Premise specific.

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.920
We want to support
Kubernetes where it mattered.

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:17.920
That was the grounding
principle on which we

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:19.240
approached the whole problem.

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:21.370
The second thing we
did was we didn't

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.702
want to yet another overlay.

00:21:23.702 --> 00:21:26.035
There are enough overlays in
these network environments.

00:21:26.035 --> 00:21:30.190
And we didn't want to yet
another overlay as much

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:32.230
as possible.

00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:34.280
But in order to be
able to support that

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:36.430
and to be able to support
multiple applications

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:39.490
without having to expose
IPs for everything,

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:42.910
we decided to separate
L4 from L7 load balancing

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:45.580
and ship L7 load balancing
as an in-built service

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:47.060
within the cluster.

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.430
So if you've used GKE
or used any cloud,

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:53.470
think about it as you
kind of assume HTTPS load

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:55.720
balancing happens for you.

00:21:55.720 --> 00:21:58.120
And then you assume there's
a service that doing that.

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:01.030
We wanted to give you that same
experience with GKE On-Prem.

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:02.635
So when you want
to get started out,

00:22:02.635 --> 00:22:05.650
you shouldn't have to think
about how L7 will work.

00:22:05.650 --> 00:22:07.540
It should just work.

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.250
And the last thing is
having done the separation,

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:13.790
we picked an Envoy-based
L7 load balancer,

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:16.810
but then decided to integrate
with your existing L4

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:19.420
solutions in your
network so that you

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:23.020
don't have to kind of invent
yet another technology for how

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:24.557
you want to do L4.

00:22:24.557 --> 00:22:26.140
So these were the
grounding principles

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:29.600
of how we approached the
problem, how we broke it down.

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:32.775
And as we look into each
of the details of how

00:22:32.775 --> 00:22:34.900
we do control plane and
data plane in the following

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:36.730
slides, but those
were the principles.

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:37.720
And as you see--

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:39.803
through these slides,
you'll see how they actually

00:22:39.803 --> 00:22:42.520
play a role in how
the solution evolves.

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:44.620
The very first thing, as
Ines was pointing out,

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:49.390
was how the control plane
itself tethers back to Google.

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:52.750
So if you think about it-- and
this was one of the very first

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:53.950
insights that we had--

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:56.080
most people just think
On-Prem and Google Cloud,

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:57.707
and they think
that's two networks.

00:22:57.707 --> 00:22:59.540
Well actually, we sat
back and looked at it.

00:22:59.540 --> 00:23:01.550
It's actually three networks.

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:03.350
There is your On-Prem network.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:07.000
There is your Google Cloud
network, which is a VPC.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:09.400
And then there is the
actual prod network

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:12.610
that Google's back
services run on.

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:14.470
And they're three
distinct networks.

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:18.400
And you own in some sense
your on-prem network

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:20.390
and your view of the cloud VPC.

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:23.050
It's your network because
it's [INAUDIBLE] project.

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:24.880
The other stuff is
Google-hosted project--

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:26.440
Google stuff.

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:28.265
So when we wanted
to tether in, we

00:23:28.265 --> 00:23:30.640
assumed that we will have to
support multiple [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:31.240
from get-go.

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.670
Like I said, we want it
to adapt to what you have.

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:36.040
So there's two ways that the
tether will actually work.

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:37.540
It could dial out
to an Egress proxy

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:39.832
that you have in your network--
because assuming you're

00:23:39.832 --> 00:23:40.630
all close networks.

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:42.790
And then do an SSL
tunnel back so you

00:23:42.790 --> 00:23:45.730
can get a secure end-to-end
connection on how your control

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:48.580
plane gets visibility on
Pantheon and your Google Cloud.

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:53.020
Or you could actually go in and,
if you have an interconnect,

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:54.685
connect through the
interconnect, which

00:23:54.685 --> 00:23:56.440
will route traffic
through your VPC

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:59.380
and stay completely
private to your enterprise

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:01.660
and never have to go
through the public internet.

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:03.035
So there's two
modes that you can

00:24:03.035 --> 00:24:07.660
use to actually go in and
register your clusters

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:09.880
into the cloud so you
can do all the management

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:12.088
of your workloads, the
marketplace access, and things

00:24:12.088 --> 00:24:13.613
like that.

00:24:13.613 --> 00:24:15.530
Now, what is it that is
registering it, right?

00:24:15.530 --> 00:24:18.682
So what is the control plane
in itself looking like?

00:24:18.682 --> 00:24:20.390
So one of the things
that we wanted to do

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:23.630
is why do we only tether a
control plane back and not

00:24:23.630 --> 00:24:25.460
run the control plane remotely?

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:29.150
We wanted to use the ability
to be able to manage and run

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:30.860
the control plane even
if you, let's say,

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:32.222
loose internet connectivity.

00:24:32.222 --> 00:24:33.430
This is your on-prem network.

00:24:33.430 --> 00:24:35.270
As long as your
on-prem network is up,

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:37.300
you should be able to
manage your clusters.

00:24:37.300 --> 00:24:42.260
And the way we did that is
by A, separating the control

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:45.200
plane from the user plane,
running a cluster just

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:47.640
for the control plane-- we
call this the admin cluster.

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:50.000
We host the workloads
for your user clusters

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:51.950
masters in the admin cluster.

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:55.050
And then we let the nodes that
are there for the user cluster

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:57.665
run just the workloads,
which is its own network.

00:24:57.665 --> 00:24:59.540
If you actually step
back and think about it,

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:02.500
this is exactly
what you get in GKE.

00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:04.990
Google manages
your master on GKE.

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:07.210
You get a bunch of nodes,
which is the user cluster

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:09.660
nodes that you scale
for your workloads.

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:12.520
You get the exact same
experience in GKE On-Prem,

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:16.420
except that the admin cluster
itself is hosted locally.

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:20.350
It builds on the same concepts,
and it exposes an L4 IP.

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:23.980
And the user cluster nodes are
completely for user workloads,

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:26.470
and they just register
back to their master

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:28.060
to do their operations.

00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:31.060
And we make all the wiring work.

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:32.372
And this then says--

00:25:32.372 --> 00:25:33.830
essentially what
it does is you run

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.980
GKE CTO to create a cluster.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:40.840
You get something in the admin
cluster for your manager.

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:43.930
And then you get all
the workloaders spun up,

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:47.800
and we go in and give you
a kubeconfig file pointing

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:48.790
to the master API.

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:51.220
And you can just do cube
ctl apply at that point,

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:55.790
and your workloads will then
run on the user cluster.

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:58.130
So that's the networking flow.

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:00.520
And in order to solve
this, we actually went in

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:02.252
and had to solve a
bunch of problems.

00:26:02.252 --> 00:26:04.460
And at this point, I'm going
to hand it over to Scott

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:05.557
to talk about--

00:26:05.557 --> 00:26:07.640
Scott actually influenced
a lot of these decisions

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:08.765
and why they were valuable.

00:26:08.765 --> 00:26:11.682
And he'll talk
about these choices.

00:26:11.682 --> 00:26:12.890
SCOTT SUROVICH: Great, great.

00:26:12.890 --> 00:26:14.060
Thank you, Satya.

00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:16.550
So as Ines said earlier,
we've been working with them

00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:18.450
for about a year now on this.

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:21.710
So when they first came
to us with the product

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:24.380
it was pretty Google
opinionated on certain things

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:27.240
that didn't fit with what
we need in the enterprise.

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:29.490
And one those first things
is that first bullet point,

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:31.850
which is the static IPAM.

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:35.530
So we also wanted to use
our own load balancers.

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:37.470
Still NF5, could
be something else.

00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:40.800
But in order to use that the way
we have to use it internally,

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:43.010
we needed static IPs.

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:45.910
Those are in a pool, so we can
still kind of leverage them.

00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:47.750
I'll call it dynamic static IPs.

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:51.020
So we can still scale out
the clusters as needed.

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:55.760
So it went from being
DHCP-based only into a option--

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:59.328
so static which is
populated through a file.

00:26:59.328 --> 00:27:01.370
So as Satya was saying,
one of the big advantages

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:03.920
we have here, when we started
our journey on Kubernetes,

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:07.560
we looked at every flavor
you could possibly think of.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:10.170
If we were to do multiple user
control planes for, let's say,

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:14.760
business, we would have multiple
masters all over the place.

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:17.400
In this case, we have
our single up there.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:20.340
All the user control planes,
I think as Satya was saying,

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:23.340
run inside there in
their own namespace.

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:25.740
And that has your API
server, your etcd servers.

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:28.050
And they are all in an HA mode.

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:31.680
That eliminates multiple
VMs that we do not

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:35.730
have to create, maintain,
patch, get IPs for.

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:37.620
So down there, it's
up to currently,

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:39.810
I believe, 10 is what
it's going to be Satya.

00:27:42.092 --> 00:27:43.550
SATYADEEP MUSUVATHY:
Thanks, Scott.

00:27:43.550 --> 00:27:48.010
And that kind of wraps up how
we thought about the control

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:50.350
plane, and essentially
give you the same flavor

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:54.850
of GKE's management
on on-prem networks,

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:58.150
giving the same separation
of basically administrator

00:27:58.150 --> 00:28:00.880
management being independent
of the user workload management

00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:03.400
and to be able to do each
of these independently.

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:05.140
And I will start
taking a look at how

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:06.680
the data plane itself works.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:08.860
So you've got the cluster up.

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:10.700
You want to deploy
your workload on it.

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:12.242
How do we actually
expect it to work?

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:16.700
Before we go into how
the networking works,

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:19.910
this is kind of like
a context of the way

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:21.980
we were trying to meet.

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:26.060
You have your on-prem
cluster, and you

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:27.850
wanted to apply some
microservices on it.

00:28:27.850 --> 00:28:30.800
And you want to expose
it through some name.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:33.110
And clients in your network--

00:28:33.110 --> 00:28:35.060
this is not like clients
out in the internet--

00:28:35.060 --> 00:28:36.980
in your on-prem prem network--

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:39.680
are trying to access each
of these internal services.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:41.750
And these services,
in some sense,

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:45.230
might want to reach outside the
cluster to talk to, let's say,

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:47.750
an existing data store or
an existing backup service

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:51.080
that you want to use and
exposed within the cluster.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:52.670
As we looked at
this picture, there

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:57.720
were like three main
requirements that bubbled up.

00:28:57.720 --> 00:28:59.930
The first is from a
security perspective

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.970
and from just an IP
utilization perspective,

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:05.300
the ability for pod IP is
not being directly reachable

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:06.342
from the on-prem network.

00:29:06.342 --> 00:29:08.450
It came out something
that's valuable.

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:11.430
So if you look at a Kubernetes
cluster, you're, let's say,

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:13.670
looking at 110 parts per node.

00:29:13.670 --> 00:29:15.740
So you want to do a
slash 24 per node.

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:18.882
You want basically
eight to 10 nodes,

00:29:18.882 --> 00:29:20.840
you'll now start looking
at slash 20, slash 80,

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:21.952
and outer spaces.

00:29:21.952 --> 00:29:23.660
And finding these
contiguous outer spaces

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:25.820
start becoming really,
really hard when you

00:29:25.820 --> 00:29:27.200
have disjointed outer spaces.

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:29.840
So by actually not having
pod IPs directly exposed,

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:31.580
you can reuse these
outer spaces and not

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:34.235
worry about losing outer spaces
in your on-prem environment

00:29:34.235 --> 00:29:35.780
to actually bring up a cluster.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:38.450
You're just using an IP
for the envelope balancer

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:39.998
and IP for the
nodes, and that's it.

00:29:39.998 --> 00:29:42.290
So it looks exactly like how
you would deploy iteration

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.035
application, except
for that cost

00:29:44.035 --> 00:29:46.660
of a traditional application you
get a full Kubernetes cluster.

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:51.640
So those are the first drivers
behind our networking model--

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:52.952
the starting networking model.

00:29:52.952 --> 00:29:55.160
Which leads to the second
point, which is essentially

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:57.440
what I was talking about,
limited number of IPs

00:29:57.440 --> 00:29:59.000
exposed to an L4 load balancer.

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:00.633
I recommend doing a
wildcard DNS match.

00:30:00.633 --> 00:30:02.300
So essentially you
want to be able to do

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.217
name-based routing instead
of IP-based routing

00:30:04.217 --> 00:30:06.350
once your traffic
hits the cluster.

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:09.110
And, finally, as Scott
was also mentioning,

00:30:09.110 --> 00:30:11.810
we gave the ability
not just to DHCP,

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:13.640
but actually a bag of
static IPs that you

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:15.182
can give to the
cluster creation tool

00:30:15.182 --> 00:30:18.740
and say, manage my nodes IPs
within this, which it lets you

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:20.330
then preconfigure
your load balancer,

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:22.163
or if you want to use
your own existing load

00:30:22.163 --> 00:30:23.570
balancers to that
shared service.

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:25.310
Because you know what
the back ends are.

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:27.540
You are basically
telling the cluster use

00:30:27.540 --> 00:30:29.840
these IPs for the nodes.

00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:33.030
So that's the context behind how
we thought about the problem.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:33.530
Scott?

00:30:36.942 --> 00:30:38.650
SCOTT SUROVICH: So,
as Satya was saying--

00:30:38.650 --> 00:30:40.480
or as Paul was also
mentioning, when

00:30:40.480 --> 00:30:42.400
we have a new service
come in, you've

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:43.570
got to get your IP address.

00:30:43.570 --> 00:30:44.920
You've got to get your DNS.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:46.503
And something we
didn't really mention

00:30:46.503 --> 00:30:48.310
is certificates
for that service.

00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:50.450
Everything has to be encrypted.

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:53.060
So with this model,
as Satya was saying,

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:54.940
we limit it to three IPs.

00:30:54.940 --> 00:30:56.440
So we're going to
need off-the-bat--

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:57.857
it can be expanded,
but it's going

00:30:57.857 --> 00:31:00.850
to be three of the Envoy-based
Ingress controllers.

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:03.790
So we use a wildcard
DNS to come in.

00:31:03.790 --> 00:31:06.250
And from there, we also
use a wildcard certificate.

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:08.110
So our developers
can spin up anything

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:10.300
that's going to be
on a Layer 7 base

00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:12.610
and instantly have access to it.

00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:16.210
And they'll do this for
a test site as well.

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:18.710
So they'll have site
A do their works.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:21.850
Site B, different
selector on the Ingress.

00:31:21.850 --> 00:31:23.590
And they can suss
out an application

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:30.990
and cut things over in a
pseudo kind of AB deployment.

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.493
And in the middle there, what
we talk about the Ingress.

00:31:33.493 --> 00:31:35.160
It might be important
to mention, Satya,

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:37.662
that we're not
stuck with just one.

00:31:37.662 --> 00:31:39.120
It does support
multiple Ingresses.

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:40.930
So Envoy-based shipping with it.

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:43.870
But if you want to put
Nginx in there as well,

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:47.010
you can do that for other
annotations that you might need

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:51.722
or other features for any
other Ingress controllers.

00:31:51.722 --> 00:31:53.180
SATYADEEP MUSUVATHY:
Thanks, Scott.

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:57.390
And in order to make that
work, what we had to do

00:31:57.390 --> 00:32:00.390
was how do we make the
pod IPs in a way that

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:01.860
meets the Kubernetes model.

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:04.950
Which, as Ines was pointing out,
assumes a flat IP address space

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:07.440
for pods within a cluster,
which is if pod A wants

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:09.870
to talk to pod B, you assume
they can directly reach

00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:11.263
each other within the cluster.

00:32:11.263 --> 00:32:12.930
But we also said we
don't want to expose

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:17.080
pod IPs outside the cluster
just to save on the IP space.

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:22.360
So what we did is we essentially
built a node-to-node BGP mesh,

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:25.380
which announces the slash 24
that's allocated to each node

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:27.660
for the pod IPs so
that they become--

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:30.990
basically, you don't need an
overlay to load the pod IPs.

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:32.490
The underlying
network will actually

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:34.140
route the pod IPs directly.

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:37.710
But since we never announced the
BGP routes outside the cluster,

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:40.350
they essentially form what
we call an island of IPs

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:42.113
within the cluster.

00:32:42.113 --> 00:32:43.530
But then this leads
to the problem

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.072
that if a pod wants to talk to
an external database, how will

00:32:46.072 --> 00:32:47.070
the traffic work?

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:51.540
What we do is if you're pod IP
is reaching outside the cluster

00:32:51.540 --> 00:32:53.850
but it's not intended
to another pod IP,

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:54.940
you hit the node boundary.

00:32:54.940 --> 00:32:59.220
We basically [INAUDIBLE]
by the nodes IP on Egress.

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:01.290
And that way the returned
route will actually

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:03.990
work with connection tracking,
which essentially then

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:06.510
gives you an island
of large IP space.

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:07.905
You can use slash
14 if you want,

00:33:07.905 --> 00:33:09.780
that you can just reuse
between each clusters

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:11.613
because they never leak
outside the cluster.

00:33:11.613 --> 00:33:14.320
And the only cost you pay is
the actual node IPs themselves,

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:17.010
which is function of the
number of nodes in a cluster.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:18.620
So this is the first thing.

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:20.550
And now given this
cluster, we look

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:22.608
at how the traffic flow works.

00:33:22.608 --> 00:33:24.900
The way the traffic flow
works is, as Scott was saying,

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:27.540
you start with a
wildcard DNS entry

00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:29.780
that you mapped to
an Ingress service.

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:31.530
We ship an ingress
service out of the box.

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:33.480
If you go through a
GKE On-Prem install,

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:36.000
it'll ask you what IP to
associate for the Ingress

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:36.760
service.

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:39.600
So you are actually telling
the system what IP to use.

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:42.300
But you can then configure
the environment to say here's

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:45.280
a wildcard DNS entry that
maps to that Ingress IP.

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:47.520
So, let's say, start
out a.b.c to make

00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:49.320
it a little more
concrete, and a client

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:51.840
wants to reach your
service S1.a.b.c.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:54.180
So the wildcard DNS
in your environment

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:55.860
that you configured
once will basically

00:33:55.860 --> 00:33:58.590
point it to the L4
load balancer on which

00:33:58.590 --> 00:34:00.558
that web was configured.

00:34:00.558 --> 00:34:02.850
The L4 load balancer will
then your standard Kubernetes

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:05.580
NodePort back ends to
send that traffic down

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:08.070
to one of the nodes in your
cluster to it's NodePort.

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:11.050
At which point, again,
standard Kubernetes takes over,

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:14.780
sends that traffic
down to the Envoy part

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:18.330
that we are running for
the Ingress service, which

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:23.520
is essentially a subset of STOs
Pilot and Envoy combination.

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:24.931
This is for [INAUDIBLE] traffic.

00:34:24.931 --> 00:34:25.889
And it's hits that pod.

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:28.080
At which point,
essentially all of this

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:30.389
is just control
plane [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:32.407
Envoy looks at the L7 header.

00:34:32.407 --> 00:34:33.449
It looks at the hostname.

00:34:33.449 --> 00:34:34.770
It looks at the pod.

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:38.489
You can deploy a standard
service with a clusterIP.

00:34:38.489 --> 00:34:41.639
These are IPs in the cluster
that never reached out.

00:34:41.639 --> 00:34:43.469
And add a Kubernetes
Ingress object

00:34:43.469 --> 00:34:46.889
that says either a pod-based
match or a hostname match,

00:34:46.889 --> 00:34:47.880
send to our service.

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:50.909
And the Envoy basically
then proxies it down

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:52.290
to your service.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:55.050
So, essentially, what this
gives you is an ability

00:34:55.050 --> 00:34:59.100
to deploy services with names
within the wildcard DNS name

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:03.843
pattern without losing any
additional IPs for each service

00:35:03.843 --> 00:35:04.510
that you put in.

00:35:04.510 --> 00:35:06.010
And you scale out
the service, scale

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:07.695
in service within the cluster.

00:35:07.695 --> 00:35:09.930
Or let's say, as Scott
was saying, your service

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:11.280
itself could be Nginx itself.

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:15.600
If you want to do your
own custom configuration,

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:18.510
all you have to do is to say,
here's the hostname match,

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:22.260
send it to my Nginx
pod or service,

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:25.770
and all your configurations
will work end-to-end.

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:28.170
So you can essentially
scale out your system

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:31.200
by having additional services
come in and be able to route

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:33.360
just by hostnames
within the cluster.

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:35.430
And your IP cost
into the network

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:38.632
is just that L4 Ingress IP
for 90% of your use cases.

00:35:38.632 --> 00:35:40.590
And we also recognize
there our other use cases

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:43.090
where you can directly use
service type load balancer.

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:45.210
But most cases you
shouldn't have to

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:46.680
if you're just
doing microservices.

00:35:46.680 --> 00:35:47.435
Scott?

00:35:47.435 --> 00:35:49.310
SCOTT SUROVICH: Yeah,
and the important thing

00:35:49.310 --> 00:35:50.700
about when this gets set up--

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:52.853
so those NodePorts
you might be thinking

00:35:52.853 --> 00:35:55.020
you have to statically
assign those over on the F5s.

00:35:55.020 --> 00:35:56.310
Let's say, OK, if
you're looking for this,

00:35:56.310 --> 00:35:59.060
you're going to go down to this
pod over here on this NodePort.

00:35:59.060 --> 00:36:02.480
There's the F5 controller
pod installed by default.

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:03.730
That handles all this for you.

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:05.813
So when it does get
installed, as Satya is saying,

00:36:05.813 --> 00:36:08.680
you basically just asked for
a couple questions-- a couple

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:10.650
being like 20, but still.

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:14.610
And it just goes off and
deploys in about 30, 35 minutes.

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:18.100
And at that point your
wildcard DNS will work.

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:21.770
You've got your pods running,
your NodePorts running.

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:24.580
And as Satya said, you're
saving all those addresses,

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:26.790
which is a big win
for us, obviously,

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:29.002
at the scale that
Paul showed you.

00:36:29.002 --> 00:36:30.210
That's a lot of IP addresses.

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.550
And that doesn't count
what's over on our load

00:36:32.550 --> 00:36:34.350
balancers, what's
behind the scenes,

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:37.900
or what's just internal
to certain other clusters.

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:40.740
So all this just helps our
developers basically do

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:45.780
their CI/CD go from nothing to
a full app in 15, 20 minutes--

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:47.157
whatever they happen to have--

00:36:47.157 --> 00:36:48.240
and everything is running.

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:50.157
So this has been very
well received internally

00:36:50.157 --> 00:36:52.160
by our application developers.

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:55.040
Just on the installation that
we have installed for Kubernetes

00:36:55.040 --> 00:36:59.060
in general, we had probably
about 115 unique namespaces

00:36:59.060 --> 00:37:01.450
within about two or
three months of use.

00:37:01.450 --> 00:37:03.590
And the feedback we're
getting from people--

00:37:03.590 --> 00:37:05.480
all of our developer
community has always

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:07.070
been positive and the speed.

00:37:10.857 --> 00:37:13.440
SATYADEEP MUSUVATHY: But then
if you realize-- if you actually

00:37:13.440 --> 00:37:16.203
sit down and think about it,
routing inside the cluster

00:37:16.203 --> 00:37:17.120
we've assumed is free.

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:19.500
We've said we've created
a mesh of all pod IPs.

00:37:19.500 --> 00:37:22.212
We've assumed all pod IPs
[INAUDIBLE] all pod IPs,

00:37:22.212 --> 00:37:23.670
which, if you're
a security person,

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:27.640
would start kind of
causing some form of worry.

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:28.925
Because how do I then protect.

00:37:28.925 --> 00:37:30.550
Because, as we've
said, we want to have

00:37:30.550 --> 00:37:32.830
multi-tenancy in some sense
between multiple services.

00:37:32.830 --> 00:37:35.040
We want to host it
within a cluster.

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:36.790
They're all routing
through the Envoy pod.

00:37:36.790 --> 00:37:38.470
And how do we lock it down?

00:37:38.470 --> 00:37:42.080
And so recognizing that,
and, as it's stable space,

00:37:42.080 --> 00:37:44.770
in most environments, we do
ship network policies out

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:46.180
of the box.

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:51.460
So any service, any
service-to-service pod-to-pod

00:37:51.460 --> 00:37:54.880
communication you can lock
it down based on your intent.

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:58.540
So, you can say, for example
in the previous slide,

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:01.900
you could potentially
say I have service S1,

00:38:01.900 --> 00:38:03.460
but that's only
reachable, let's say,

00:38:03.460 --> 00:38:06.632
from the Istio service
for the Envoy pod

00:38:06.632 --> 00:38:08.590
and not by any other
service within the cluster

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:09.430
if you wanted to.

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:12.520
Or the back info service one is
only reachable by service one.

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:15.190
And so even if somebody wanted
to write an Ingress rule that

00:38:15.190 --> 00:38:18.140
said expose it as a new
name, the network policy

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:21.250
won't even the traffic
down to that service.

00:38:21.250 --> 00:38:22.890
So these are supported
out of the box.

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:23.390
Scott?

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:25.140
SCOTT SUROVICH: Yeah,
the network policies

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:27.400
have been a big request
from our developer community

00:38:27.400 --> 00:38:30.280
for alternate solutions in
[INAUDIBLE] environments

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:32.770
or even in full VM mode.

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:34.995
And it's very difficult,
especially in a full VM

00:38:34.995 --> 00:38:38.360
mode to let a developer
log in and say, OK,

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:40.660
this server can only
go to A, B, or, C.

00:38:40.660 --> 00:38:42.160
So it's been
something that we've

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:43.543
needed for a very long time.

00:38:43.543 --> 00:38:45.460
And I know we have some
of our security people

00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:46.377
in this room actually.

00:38:46.377 --> 00:38:49.840
So I know this is something
that they're very happy about.

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:52.960
So it gives the developer the
ability to configure this.

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:55.510
It's just standard Kubernetes
network policies, which

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:57.190
many of you have probably seen.

00:38:57.190 --> 00:38:59.920
So you can control your
Ingress and your Egress

00:38:59.920 --> 00:39:03.340
based on protocol,
IPs, destinations.

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:04.840
So this is something
that we've been

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:07.830
asked by a lot of our
businesses on how am I

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:09.130
going to secure this.

00:39:09.130 --> 00:39:11.560
And I did mention on
the previous slide

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:14.230
that we have 120,
130 namespaces.

00:39:14.230 --> 00:39:17.720
So obviously, multi-tenant
is one of our concerns.

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:21.190
This is something that helps
to secure that multi-tenancy.

00:39:21.190 --> 00:39:23.467
So we can have only the
applications talking to what

00:39:23.467 --> 00:39:24.550
they should be talking to.

00:39:29.505 --> 00:39:31.130
So, with that, what
we're going to do--

00:39:31.130 --> 00:39:33.130
